  "description": "Here is how to read the Gemma 4 E4B config.json. The architecture class is Gemma4ForConditionalGeneration, which is the transformers (or equivalent) module that wires embeddings, attention, the MLP, and the language-modelling head that turns hidden states into next-token logits.\n\nhidden_size is 2,560: that is the width of every residual stream vector. The embedding matrix is therefore 262,144 \u00d7 2,560: each token id owns a vector of that width.\n\nnum_attention_heads is 8, so each head sees 256 dimensions (2,560 / 8). The ratio test is hidden_size / num_attention_heads = 2560 / 8 = 256. 256 is a multiple of 32 (NVIDIA warp-friendly), and a multiple of 16 (Huawei cube-friendly), and a power of two (Triton / FlashAttention-friendly). From this number alone you cannot name the training chip.\n\nnum_key_value_heads is 2, so this is grouped-query attention (GQA): 8 query heads share 2 key/value groups, so the KV cache is about 4\u00d7 smaller than full MHA.\n\nnum_hidden_layers is 42: the stack is that many transformer blocks deep.\n\nintermediate_size is 10,240. In a SwiGLU MLP this is the high-dimensional workspace: the 2,560-wide stream is projected up, activated with gelu_pytorch_tanh, then projected back. Labs usually round this width to a multiple of 128 or 256 so it tiles on the chip.\n\nmax_position_embeddings is 131,072 tokens \u2014 the context window in the config. There is no rope_scaling entry, so the rotary positions are used as-is.\n\nvocab_size is 262,144. 262,144 is divisible by 256, the usual vocab-tiling grain. bos/eos ids, when present, are just two special rows in that table; the padded vocab is often larger than the tokenizer\u2019s real inventory.\n\ntie_word_embeddings is true: the same matrix is reused for input embeddings and the LM head.\n\ntorch_dtype is bfloat16. Brain floating point keeps FP32\u2019s exponent range with less mantissa, which avoids the overflow/underflow of a tiny exponent at the cost of noisier decimals \u2014 and it halves the on-chip footprint versus FP32. NVIDIA A100s were the first NVIDIA chips with native BF16; Huawei Ascend 910-class parts also prefer BF16/FP16 on the cube.\n\nThe copy of config.json is from https://huggingface.co/google/Gemma-4-E4B/blob/main/config.json."
